hi welcome to architecting with google  cloud i'm gabe weiss  shared file storage is the unsung hero  of application development pick the  right solution chances are no one's  going to notice but pick the wrong  solution and you'll be chasing  bottlenecks for days no one knows this  better than our partners over at netapp  and today i've got oliver krause  technical marketing engineer from netapp  to tell us about a great solution for  windows file sharing oliver why don't  you take a second introduce yourself and  tell us a little bit about what you and  netapp do  hi gabe thanks for having me so i'm  oliver located in germany near munich  i over 20 years ago i started at a  storage startup called netapp  it became a unicorn and today it's one  of the biggest storage and data  management companies in the world  i held many different positions in that  company usually in pre-sales positions  helping customers solve their storage  problems  and few years ago i  switched to into our cloud business  simply to help do new stuff in the cloud  world glad to have you so oliver i've  got a windows application and i want to  run it in the cloud but i know there are  a ton of file storage solutions out  there maybe you could tell us just a  little bit about what's out there and  some pros and cons  yeah in the storage world we  have three different kinds of storages  the first one is a traditional disk like  a hard disk or an ssd or usb stick it's  basically a disk which you plug into  into your computer put the file system  on top of it and use it locally it's  also called block storage  after some time people thought oh it  would be cool if we could  use that file system on that disk on  multiple computers so share the data  and don't share it on a disk base but  share it on a per file base so basically  and file sharing was invented some file  sharing file systems were indented and  people were able to use data on multiple  vms or multiple computers in that time  and the new kit on the block is object  storage like google cloud storage  it uses http to store data  relatively new let's say 10 years or  something  and it's a great way to store  unstructured data  but most of the enterprise applications  in enterprise data centers  still don't talk object  but they talk  file nas and to share data  so people just say like just lift and  ship it's so easy but clearly if your  application doesn't speak object storage  that's not going to be so easy so you've  been around  what specific challenges have you seen  in terms of shifting a windows  application up to the cloud okay yeah so  let's assume we want to do a lift and  shift with a windows application  and that application requires data  sharing between multiple vms  which is by way a very common scenario  to enable that  you first need to deploy a gce instance  with windows the operating system  running  you need to size vm correctly  eventually you have to patch it keep up  the latest patches you have to monitor  it  maybe the performance requirements  change so you have to resize it  you have to think about backup and  recovery  and maybe even even make it high  available so multiple zones and so on  and after you did all of that you then  you can start to attach a data disk put  a file system on on top of it and share  it via the smb protocol to other  machines so hang on you mentioned smb  some folks might not necessarily know  what smb is could you just explain real  quick what that is and maybe you know  compared to what folks might be more  familiar with like nfs yeah so to share  out the local file system which is on a  disk  in a computer if you want to share it  out to different computers  you need a protocol to access the file  system over a network  in the old days  most systems have been unix  and nfs was invented net network file  system  which is still used in  unix-based or linux-based systems today  and very popular  in the windows world microsoft invented  smb which is a  abbreviation for server message block in  the beginning it was not a very good  protocol  but anyway  in the 2000s microsoft renamed it to  cifs sifs which stands for common  internet file system you know everything  had to be named internet something  later on um microsoft renamed it back to  smb  today we have smb version 2.1 and very  recently free which are very good and  capable protocols got it and i know  cloud volume services or cvs for short  supports both smb and  nfs so this leads nicely into what we  want to talk about today so to start why  don't you just tell us what is cvs cvs  is nas shares as a service  you create a volume  and share it via nfs or smb or both  to your clients  and since the service is super easy to  use and to consume and trouble free to  manage and how does cps help us  specifically with like a windows  migration for example yeah like i said  if you  do it  with a windows way you have to manage a  windows vm with all the edge  adjacent problems  cvs  in contrast is a service  so you can easily  create volumes you can scale them up and  scale them down both capacity and  performance wise  just you pay  per  gigabyte per month just like you do with  persistent disks and gcs  you get a build from google instead of  netapp because it's built through google  it's google takes you support calls so  it's supported by google  and additionally  we have snapshots built in and these  snapshots are different from the  persistent disk snapshots you might be  used to  so let me quickly elaborate on these  snapshots and give you free use cases  these snapshots are  instant to do within a second you  created a snapshot which is basically a  photo of your file system  you can do multiple of them  and with that you can build a history of  your file system like a versioning  system  and the first use case let's assume you  want to upgrade an application and  you're concerned that this upgrade might  fail and it changes your data and you're  left with an unfinished upgrade which  changed your data  you simply do a snapshot which is super  fast it's like creating a backup in in a  second  you do the upgrade if it works fine  delete the snapshot afterwards  if it fails  sim you have the snapshot and can easily  restore your data to the last known good  state the second use cage case which is  my likely the most relevant one for a  lot of customers  is  [Music]  you can enhance your backup and recovery  processes with snapshots  simply build a snapshot schedule which  takes a snapshot every day for the last  few weeks  and if you delete that file you can  simply go back  to that snapshot with a windows file  explorer so the user itself can do it in  the ui  they can simply go in there and identify  the last good version of the file and  restore it back to their file system  so it helps them  avoid a head death ticket and nobody  likes helped hit that ticket right  and the restore is much much faster  and you can even go beyond that  since snapshots are so lightweight and  cheap to do  simply take them every hour  take hourly snapshots and if you now  delete a file you not only get reversion  from yesterday from the nightly backup  you can get the version from last hour  which is way more current so that's a  super cool functionality  to reduce the recovery point objective  and the last use case  since we are talking windows operating  systems here  the issue with windows is  where  in danger that ransomware  infects them and encrypts all the files  on your disk  if you have since you have snapshots you  can take them every hour or so and you  have a very recent  known good state where your files not  have been infected  and the cool thing is you can really  quickly restore that files individually  or you can  roll back the whole volume  within  a few seconds half a minute or so  you can roll it back to the state of the  last known snapshot and basically delete  all the encrypted files by that  super cool and last thing  it doesn't matter how big your file  system is be it a terabyte or 100  terabyte  the time it takes to create a snapshot  or roll back a snapshot it's all the  same these snapshots sound really cool  can you can you like make copies of them  can you clone them into like other  for other applications yeah you can  simply  create a snapshot of a volume and then  create a new volume from that snapshot  and by the way this process also takes a  few seconds  let's say you were done in half a minute  or less yeah the so the developer in me  is imagining you know as you're talking  kind of popped into my head of uh you  know we oftentimes as application  developers run into a situation where  you've got a very specific configuration  of files on disk that reproduce a  problem for your applications and  sometimes reproducing that is kind of  hard but if you're making these  snapshots i can imagine a place where  you get a file you know a file system  set up where it reproduces the problem  you snapshot it so that you can make  changes and then reset to be able to  reproduce the problem that's super cool  so oliver i'd love to dive in a little  bit deeper here could you walk through  maybe where we can find it and then a  use case or two where we'd want to use  it yeah it's a good good thing is it's  directly built into the gcp console just  open the gcp console search for cloud  volumes  and it's where it's also on the left  side if you look up all the services you  will find it down at the bottom under  partner services  you will also find the documentation in  the cloud architecture center on  cloudgoogle.com regarding use cases i  want to present two the first use case  is a pretty generic architecture which  is used on thousands of legacy  applications  you have multiple gcv vms running  windows applications and they need a  file share to share data  so what you do is you simply provision a  cvs share and map it to the windows  systems  and we have multiple examples  where this applies the first one would  be for example vdi virtual desktop  infrastructure  where you have  smb shares for the user home directories  for group directories where people share  data between multiple users  and for the profile shares  another one would be automotive and  manufacturing  they're running a lot of computer-aided  design and computer-aided engineering  applications  most of them want to talk to a file  system to share data  so an smb share is the way to go here  and that applies to many other  industry verticals and the last one i  want to mention is media and  entertainment more and more we see that  these people have geographically  dispersed teams working together on a  project  and they all need to access the same  data when same media assets in a  performant and  consistent way to aid these requirements  we have a solution called global file  cache which builds on top of cloud  volume service  it helps you to share out  a share  globally to all the different sites for  example l.a london singapore  while retaining excess performance over  the wear network  on the other side minimizing traffic to  reduce costs  and enabling global file locking to  avoid conflicts when multiple users  tried to modify the same file at the  same time this is all really great thank  you so much oliver for being here today  and talking through this great  partnership between google cloud and  netapp using smb file share on windows  for windows application development it's  really been a pleasure having you here  yeah my pleasure thanks for having me  gabe  and if you all out there want to dive in  right away we've got links in the  description below to send more great  content to see this in action if you all  have any more ideas for customer stories  or architecture that you're interested  in learning more about let us know in  the comments and if you want to see more  of this kind of content click the like  and subscribe buttons see you next time  [Music]  you

